<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Composting Is Easy. – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Recycling for Our World</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Composting Is Easy.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write short reports on classroom experiments or projects. Interactive: Write phrases or sentences for a group story or project with support or guidance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a simple report on a project to describe the activity and its purpose (e.g., "Our poster is about recycling plastic bottles." "We explain how to recycle paper and bottles."). Can write sentences about recycling as part of a group experiment or project (e.g., "We recycle paper."). Can describe what they learned (e.g., "Recycling helps the Earth.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of items related to composting (e.g., food scraps, banana peel, leaves, plastic bottle, glass jar). Students quickly identify the items and say "Compost!" or "Trash!" The teacher guides students to say simple sentences like "We can compost food scraps." or "We cannot compost plastic." This activates vocabulary and sets the theme.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple sentence on the board: "We compost food scraps to help the Earth." The teacher models writing the sentence, emphasizing clear handwriting. The teacher then models the pronunciation of /r/ in "recycle" and /s/ in "sustainability" by saying words like "recycle," "reuse," "save," "sustainable" and exaggerating the sounds. For phonemic awareness, the teacher writes "environment" and "sustainability" on the board, claps out the syllables, and segments the words verbally to demonstrate: "en-vi-ron-ment" and "sus-tain-a-bil-i-ty."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we going to write long stories or short sentences today?" (Expected answer: Short sentences.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What words can we use for composting? Can you tell me one?" (Expected answer: Food scraps / environment / recycle / save, etc.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Recycling for Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple, illustrated report or set of instructions about a classroom composting project or a school recycling program. (Genre: short report/instructions, Register: informal/educational, Format: A short paragraph with 3-4 simple sentences on a poster or handout).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Text: "Our school has a recycling program. We can recycle paper and plastic bottles. There is a blue bin for paper and a green bin for plastic. Recycling helps the environment. It is easy!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The ability to understand basic written information about environmental actions and identify key details in a simple report or instructional text to serve as a model for their own writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the short report silently. The teacher reads it aloud.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is this text about?" (Expected answer: Recycling / our school program).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False activity:</b> Students write T or F next to statements: "Our school recycles glass." (F) "There is a blue bin for paper." (T) "Recycling is difficult." (F)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences using present simple, modal verbs "can/could," and "there is/there are" with target vocabulary (e.g., plastic, bottles, compost, recycling, trash, environment, food scraps, bin, recycle, save, help, easy, green).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks. Students complete them using vocabulary words from a word bank (e.g., recycle, compost, bin, easy, environment, save, food scraps).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "We can _________ paper." (recycle) "Composting is _________." (easy) "There is a _________ for _________ _________." (bin / food scraps) "We _________ the _________." (save / environment)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Sentence Scramble and Extension:</b> The teacher writes jumbled words on the board (e.g., "recycle / we / plastic / can / bottles"). Students reorder the words to form correct sentences. Then, the teacher asks them to add one more relevant word. (e.g., "We can recycle plastic bottles in the blue bin."). This encourages writing longer, more descriptive sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Sentence Exchange and Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student writes three simple sentences about what they can recycle or compost at home or school, using "We can..." "There is..." or present simple verbs. Then, they exchange notebooks with their partner. Partners check each other's sentences for correct vocabulary and basic grammar. They can give a thumbs up if it's correct or point to a word if it needs checking.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Recycling for Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided Group Report Writing on a "Classroom Composting/Recycling Project". Students will work in small groups (3-4 students) to collaboratively write a short, simple report (3-5 sentences) about an imagined or real classroom composting or recycling project. The teacher provides a writing frame or guiding questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students will write about "Composting Is Easy." by describing what their class *can* do or *does* to recycle or compost, connecting to "Recycling for Our World."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides the class into small groups and provides a simple template on a large sheet of paper or whiteboard.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guiding Questions for their report:</b> What is our project about? What can we recycle/compost? Where do we put the items? Why do we do it? What did we learn?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups discuss and then collaboratively write their short report. Each group member contributes at least one sentence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, providing support and vocabulary as needed. Encourages use of "We can recycle...", "There is a bin...", "Recycling helps...", "It is easy!". Pragmatic function of "Giving reasons: We recycle to help the planet" is practiced here.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After writing, groups can read their reports aloud to the class (briefly) or display them.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during group work and a simple checklist/rubric for the written group report.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group submits their collaboratively written short report about their classroom composting/recycling project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 3 target vocabulary words (e.g., compost, recycle, environment, food scraps).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses present simple tense correctly in at least 2 sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "can" or "there is/are" correctly in at least 1 sentence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The report describes an activity and its purpose clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Report is legible and sentences are easy to understand.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Individual):</b> Students complete two sentences on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I learned to write about..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write sentences using the words: _________ and _________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (oral or written):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for you about writing sentences today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about writing about composting or recycling?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., student collaboration, use of sentence frames, specific vocabulary practice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with sentence formation or vocabulary usage? (e.g., more sentence starters, picture prompts, peer support).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The collaborative reports from this lesson will be integrated into the 21st Century Skills Project in Lesson 5, where students will present their ideas. For Lesson 5, students will use their written reports to create a visual presentation (e.g., a poster) and briefly explain their project, which will involve mediation and integration of all skills.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>